1
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:01,670
Thirty thousand, four hundred and eight.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:05,050
That's how many Jeeps
Australians bought in 2014.

3
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,230
In the first half of
2026, Jeep sold just 365.

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:12,190
In June, it sold twenty-eight
in the whole country.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,390
Jeep isn't alone.

6
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,750
Peugeot's Australian distributor
has walked away from the brand.

7
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,410
Jaguar sold zero new cars
here in September 2026.

8
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,350
Alfa Romeo is going backwards.

9
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,110
And some of the biggest
Japanese names Mitsubishi,

10
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:28,850
Subaru and Nissan have seen their sales
fall by around a quarter in a single

11
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,140
year, while facing millions of
dollars in emissions penalties.

12
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:35,980
Meanwhile, Australia now has around
seventy car brands fighting for roughly

13
00:00:36,030 --> 00:00:37,890
1.2 million sales a year.

14
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:41,090
The head of Australia's car dealer
association has already warned that more

15
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:42,130
brands will leave.

16
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:43,070
So in this video,

17
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:46,150
I'll go through the car brands that are
under the most pressure heading into

18
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:49,770
2027, using real 2026 sales data.

19
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:53,130
I'll explain what the companies themselves
are saying because none of them has

20
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:54,490
announced it's leaving.

21
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:55,610
And most importantly,

22
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:57,690
I'll show you exactly what
happens to your warranty,

23
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:01,830
servicing, parts and resale value
if your brand does pull out.

24
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:04,450
Stay to the end, because if
you own one of these cars,

25
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:05,980
there are things you should do now.

26
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:08,289
First, the big picture.

27
00:01:08,339 --> 00:01:10,489
Why are so many brands struggling at once?

28
00:01:10,539 --> 00:01:12,519
Reason one: the Chinese brands.

29
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:15,209
In the first half of 2026,

30
00:01:15,259 --> 00:01:21,336
according to CarExpert, BYD sales
rose 124 per cent to 52,335.

31
00:01:21,386 --> 00:01:24,389
Chery was up almost
seventy-seven per cent.

32
00:01:24,439 --> 00:01:27,089
Tesla was up almost sixty-seven per cent.

33
00:01:27,139 --> 00:01:29,249
GWM was up about twenty per cent.

34
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:31,389
In September 2026,

35
00:01:31,439 --> 00:01:35,229
Geely was up 870 per cent on
the same month a year earlier,

36
00:01:35,279 --> 00:01:38,349
and Omoda Jaecoo was up 344 per cent.

37
00:01:38,399 --> 00:01:41,096
Every one of those sales
has to come from somewhere.

38
00:01:41,146 --> 00:01:44,329
Reason two: the government's
emissions rules.

39
00:01:44,379 --> 00:01:46,389
Under the New Vehicle Efficiency Standard,

40
00:01:46,439 --> 00:01:49,689
brands with too many high-emitting
vehicles build up penalties.

41
00:01:49,739 --> 00:01:51,789
In the first six months of the scheme,

42
00:01:51,839 --> 00:01:55,869
Mazda's estimated liability
was around $25.4 million,

43
00:01:55,919 --> 00:01:58,429
Nissan's around $10.7 million,

44
00:01:58,479 --> 00:02:02,309
Subaru's almost $7 million and
Hyundai's about $4.2 million.

45
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:05,536
And the targets get much tougher in 2027.

46
00:02:05,586 --> 00:02:08,849
Reason three: the sheer number of brands.

47
00:02:08,899 --> 00:02:12,769
With around seventy brands in a
market of about 1.2 million sales,

48
00:02:12,819 --> 00:02:15,369
there simply isn't enough room
for everyone to make money.

49
00:02:15,419 --> 00:02:17,789
And it's not just the
smaller brands feeling it.

50
00:02:17,839 --> 00:02:22,319
Even Toyota still by far the biggest
brand in the country was down 21.4 per

51
00:02:22,369 --> 00:02:24,629
cent in the first half of 2026,

52
00:02:24,679 --> 00:02:29,469
to 95,141 sales, according to CarExpert.

53
00:02:29,519 --> 00:02:31,549
Suzuki was down almost
twenty-one per cent.

54
00:02:31,599 --> 00:02:33,889
Porsche was down almost thirty per cent,

55
00:02:33,939 --> 00:02:37,949
Cupra more than thirty-two per cent
and Maserati thirty-three per cent.

56
00:02:37,999 --> 00:02:40,229
When the market leader is down by a fifth,

57
00:02:40,279 --> 00:02:42,749
the brands at the bottom have
very little room to move.

58
00:02:42,799 --> 00:02:44,429
Back in late 2024,

59
00:02:44,479 --> 00:02:47,649
the Australian Automotive Dealer
Association's chief executive,

60
00:02:47,699 --> 00:02:51,129
James Voortman, predicted: "I
think we will see a number leave,

61
00:02:51,179 --> 00:02:54,509
some of them might not necessarily
make the front pages." He said the real

62
00:02:54,559 --> 00:02:57,949
question was "how many important brands
are going to leave." And he pointed

63
00:02:57,999 --> 00:03:01,009
out that during his time
leading the association,

64
00:03:01,059 --> 00:03:05,149
Australia had already lost Infiniti,
Chrysler, Citroen and Opel.

65
00:03:05,199 --> 00:03:08,989
Before that, of course, there was
Holden the biggest exit of all, in 2020.

66
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:11,369
Brands leaving Australia isn't a theory.

67
00:03:11,419 --> 00:03:12,649
It happens regularly.

68
00:03:12,699 --> 00:03:15,569
Now, a very important point
before we start the list.

69
00:03:15,619 --> 00:03:19,069
None of the brands in this video has
announced that it's leaving Australia.

70
00:03:19,119 --> 00:03:20,509
Several have said the opposite.

71
00:03:20,559 --> 00:03:22,669
So this isn't a list of
brands that are leaving.

72
00:03:22,719 --> 00:03:25,969
It's a list of brands under real pressure
and what you should know if you own

73
00:03:26,019 --> 00:03:26,819
one.

74
00:03:27,288 --> 00:03:30,518
Let's start with the one in the
most trouble on paper: Jeep.

75
00:03:30,568 --> 00:03:35,378
In 2014, Jeep sold 30,408
vehicles in Australia.

76
00:03:35,428 --> 00:03:36,538
That was its peak.

77
00:03:36,588 --> 00:03:40,298
In 2024, it sold 2,456.

78
00:03:40,348 --> 00:03:43,738
In 2025, 1,682.

79
00:03:43,788 --> 00:03:45,558
And in the first half of 2026,

80
00:03:45,608 --> 00:03:51,448
CarExpert reported just 365 sales down
more than sixty-six per cent with only

81
00:03:51,498 --> 00:03:52,838
twenty-eight in June.

82
00:03:52,888 --> 00:03:55,338
In September 2026, it sold sixteen.

83
00:03:55,388 --> 00:03:57,548
The dealer network has shrunk too.

84
00:03:57,598 --> 00:04:00,598
CarExpert reported around
forty-four Jeep retailers,

85
00:04:00,648 --> 00:04:02,838
down from more than seventy at its peak.

86
00:04:02,888 --> 00:04:04,498
And back in 2025,

87
00:04:04,548 --> 00:04:07,918
some Jeep dealers sought legal advice
from the same lawyer who represented

88
00:04:07,968 --> 00:04:10,598
Holden dealers when General
Motors closed Holden.

89
00:04:10,648 --> 00:04:12,498
And look at what's left of the range.

90
00:04:12,548 --> 00:04:15,738
Jeep's current Australian line-up
is the Avenger electric SUV,

91
00:04:15,788 --> 00:04:18,638
the Compass, the Wrangler
and the Gladiator ute.

92
00:04:18,688 --> 00:04:23,398
The Grand Cherokee, which
sold 16,582 cars in 2014,

93
00:04:23,448 --> 00:04:29,338
sold just 645 in 2024 its last full
year on sale before it was paused.

94
00:04:29,388 --> 00:04:30,818
Prices are part of the problem.

95
00:04:30,868 --> 00:04:35,618
In 2025, CarSauce reported that the
cheapest four-door Wrangler had risen from

96
00:04:35,668 --> 00:04:41,838
about $37,000 in 2014 to
$75,950 before on-road

97
00:04:41,888 --> 00:04:42,698
costs.

98
00:04:42,748 --> 00:04:47,258
And Jeep paused the Grand Cherokee in
March 2025 a model that sold more than

99
00:04:47,308 --> 00:04:49,078
16,000 a year at its peak.

100
00:04:49,128 --> 00:04:50,558
But Jeep insists it's staying.

101
00:04:50,608 --> 00:04:52,498
In April 2026,

102
00:04:52,548 --> 00:04:54,818
Jeep's vice-president of
global product planning,

103
00:04:54,868 --> 00:04:57,198
Matt Nyquist, told CarExpert:

104
00:04:57,248 --> 00:05:00,478
"The right-hand drive markets are so
important… we're committed to right-hand

105
00:05:00,528 --> 00:05:04,478
drive [and] Australia." Jeep says
a new Compass is due in 2026,

106
00:05:04,528 --> 00:05:09,038
and the electric Recon is "on the horizon."
Nyquist did acknowledge that plans

107
00:05:09,088 --> 00:05:13,108
can change, and that Jeep constantly
reviews mature markets like Australia.

108
00:05:13,966 --> 00:05:17,016
It's a sad story for a brand that
was once one of the biggest names in

109
00:05:17,066 --> 00:05:18,816
Australian four-wheel driving.

110
00:05:18,866 --> 00:05:23,116
In the 2010s, a Grand Cherokee was
a common sight in every suburb.

111
00:05:23,166 --> 00:05:26,816
Today, many Australians under thirty
have probably never set foot in a Jeep

112
00:05:26,866 --> 00:05:27,383
showroom.

113
00:05:27,433 --> 00:05:28,876
Number two: Peugeot.

114
00:05:28,926 --> 00:05:35,316
Peugeot's best year in Australia was
2007, when it sold 8,807 vehicles.

115
00:05:35,366 --> 00:05:38,696
In 2025, it sold 1,350.

116
00:05:38,746 --> 00:05:40,996
In the first half of 2026,

117
00:05:41,046 --> 00:05:45,636
CarExpert reported 427 sales,
down more than forty-one per cent.

118
00:05:45,686 --> 00:05:48,676
And in September 2026,
it sold just eighty-nine.

119
00:05:48,726 --> 00:05:51,076
And look at what Peugeot
is actually selling.

120
00:05:51,126 --> 00:05:56,536
CarExpert reported that of Peugeot's
373 deliveries to the end of May 2026,

121
00:05:56,586 --> 00:06:00,456
161 about forty-three per
cent were the Partner van.

122
00:06:00,506 --> 00:06:02,876
Its passenger cars are barely registering.

123
00:06:02,926 --> 00:06:04,916
Among Stellantis brands in Australia,

124
00:06:04,966 --> 00:06:12,216
Peugeot was third this year, behind
Ram with 1,257 sales and Fiat with 745.

125
00:06:12,266 --> 00:06:15,796
Then, in July 2026, came the big blow.

126
00:06:15,846 --> 00:06:19,776
Inchcape, the company that has imported
and distributed Peugeot in Australia,

127
00:06:19,826 --> 00:06:22,216
agreed with Stellantis
to end the partnership.

128
00:06:22,266 --> 00:06:26,396
Inchcape had already dropped Peugeot's
sister brand Citroen in 2024.

129
00:06:26,446 --> 00:06:27,956
No final date has been set.

130
00:06:28,006 --> 00:06:30,036
Stellantis says Peugeot is staying.

131
00:06:30,086 --> 00:06:32,876
Its statement: "Peugeot has
a strong future in Australia,

132
00:06:32,926 --> 00:06:37,416
supported by a robust product pipeline
and a clear long-term strategy." It

133
00:06:37,466 --> 00:06:41,316
said it intends to "maintain continuity
of distribution in Australia." But it

134
00:06:41,366 --> 00:06:45,176
hasn't said who will actually distribute
the brand once Inchcape steps away.

135
00:06:45,226 --> 00:06:46,276
In the meantime,

136
00:06:46,326 --> 00:06:49,916
Inchcape will keep selling existing
stock and handling warranty and servicing

137
00:06:49,966 --> 00:06:51,256
for current owners.

138
00:06:51,306 --> 00:06:54,976
And Peugeot has slashed
prices in July 2026,

139
00:06:55,026 --> 00:06:59,816
The Beep reported the 5008 GT Premium
Hybrid cut by ten thousand dollars,

140
00:06:59,866 --> 00:07:05,926
from $69,990 to $59,990 drive-away.

141
00:07:06,696 --> 00:07:08,246
Number three: Jaguar.

142
00:07:08,296 --> 00:07:09,026
This one is different,

143
00:07:09,076 --> 00:07:12,946
because Jaguar isn't really struggling
with sales it has deliberately stopped

144
00:07:12,996 --> 00:07:14,646
selling new cars.

145
00:07:14,696 --> 00:07:18,226
Jaguar stopped selling new petrol cars
in Australia on the 31st of December

146
00:07:18,276 --> 00:07:22,666
2025, as it prepares to relaunch
as an electric luxury brand.

147
00:07:22,716 --> 00:07:26,686
That's why it sold zero new
cars here in September 2026.

148
00:07:26,736 --> 00:07:30,126
The new electric Jaguars are
expected to cost around $300,000,

149
00:07:30,176 --> 00:07:31,646
according to Man of Many.

150
00:07:31,696 --> 00:07:34,186
And there's still no
confirmed local launch date.

151
00:07:34,236 --> 00:07:37,706
Jaguar's local retailers are
still servicing existing cars.

152
00:07:37,756 --> 00:07:40,586
But for a brand with nearly
a century of history here,

153
00:07:40,636 --> 00:07:44,426
going from a full range to
zero new cars is a huge gamble.

154
00:07:44,476 --> 00:07:48,746
If you own a Jaguar, the practical
questions are servicing, parts and resale.

155
00:07:48,796 --> 00:07:51,506
Existing Jaguars are still
supported by local retailers.

156
00:07:51,556 --> 00:07:53,506
But with no new models on sale,

157
00:07:53,556 --> 00:07:57,426
there are fewer buyers walking into
showrooms and that can make selling a used

158
00:07:57,476 --> 00:08:00,206
Jaguar harder, at least until
the new electric models arrive.

159
00:08:00,256 --> 00:08:03,086
Number four: Alfa Romeo.

160
00:08:03,136 --> 00:08:07,966
Alfa Romeo sold just twenty-one
cars in Australia in September 2026.

161
00:08:08,016 --> 00:08:11,686
By May, its sales were down almost
forty-seven per cent for the year.

162
00:08:11,736 --> 00:08:15,266
For a brand with such a passionate
following, that's a tiny number.

163
00:08:15,316 --> 00:08:17,386
Alfa's range in Australia
has been small for years,

164
00:08:17,436 --> 00:08:19,646
and when a brand only
has a couple of models,

165
00:08:19,696 --> 00:08:22,826
one slow model can drag
down the whole business.

166
00:08:22,876 --> 00:08:23,946
Like Jeep and Peugeot,

167
00:08:23,996 --> 00:08:26,286
it's part of Stellantis and as we'll see,

168
00:08:26,336 --> 00:08:29,476
the whole Stellantis group in
Australia is under pressure.

169
00:08:30,243 --> 00:08:33,273
Before we get to the big Japanese
brands which is where this gets really

170
00:08:33,323 --> 00:08:35,253
interesting a quick favour.

171
00:08:35,303 --> 00:08:37,213
Do you own a car from one of these brands?

172
00:08:37,263 --> 00:08:40,173
Has your dealer closed, or have
you had trouble getting parts?

173
00:08:40,223 --> 00:08:41,513
Tell me in the comments.

174
00:08:41,563 --> 00:08:42,533
And if you want honest,

175
00:08:42,583 --> 00:08:46,733
fact-checked videos about what's
changing for Aussie drivers in 2027,

176
00:08:46,783 --> 00:08:47,673
hit subscribe.

177
00:08:47,723 --> 00:08:49,483
It's free and it really helps.

178
00:08:50,290 --> 00:08:51,820
Now, the Japanese brands.

179
00:08:51,870 --> 00:08:55,320
Let's be very clear: these brands
are not about to leave Australia.

180
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:57,480
They sell tens of
thousands of cars a year,

181
00:08:57,530 --> 00:09:00,020
and they have huge dealer
and service networks.

182
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:03,460
But they're under more pressure than at
any time in years and that can affect

183
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:06,260
prices, models and resale values.

184
00:09:06,310 --> 00:09:11,600
Mitsubishi: 24,802 sales
in the first half of 2026,

185
00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:14,600
down 25.7 per cent,
according to CarExpert.

186
00:09:14,650 --> 00:09:21,000
In September 2026, it was down 41.5 per
cent on the same month a year earlier.

187
00:09:21,050 --> 00:09:23,840
The good news for Mitsubishi
is the all-new Pajero,

188
00:09:23,890 --> 00:09:30,920
due in showrooms from December 2026,
priced from $68,490 before on-road costs.

189
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:36,620
Subaru: 14,817 sales in the
first half, down 25.6 per cent.

190
00:09:36,670 --> 00:09:39,540
In September, down 42.6 per cent.

191
00:09:39,590 --> 00:09:44,470
Subaru's estimated emissions liability
for the second half of 2025 was almost

192
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:46,670
seven million dollars.

193
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:50,140
Nissan: 13,854 sales in the first half,

194
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:52,700
with double-digit falls
across its main models.

195
00:09:52,750 --> 00:09:57,000
Its estimated emissions liability
was around $10.7 million.

196
00:09:57,050 --> 00:09:59,760
Nissan's Oceania managing
director, Steve Milette,

197
00:09:59,810 --> 00:10:00,620
has openly said:

198
00:10:00,670 --> 00:10:05,060
"2026 is the low point." Nissan
is betting on new models,

199
00:10:05,110 --> 00:10:09,980
including the plug-in hybrid Navara
Pro for 2027 and the new V6 Patrol.

200
00:10:10,030 --> 00:10:13,760
And Mazda: the biggest estimated
emissions liability of any brand,

201
00:10:13,810 --> 00:10:17,500
at around $25.4 million
for just six months.

202
00:10:17,550 --> 00:10:19,480
Mazda needs electric and hybrid cars,

203
00:10:19,530 --> 00:10:23,100
and fast including the CX-6e electric SUV.

204
00:10:23,150 --> 00:10:25,620
Why does this matter if you
own one of these brands?

205
00:10:25,670 --> 00:10:28,880
Because when a brand is under
pressure, it has to make tough choices.

206
00:10:28,930 --> 00:10:31,940
It might raise prices on its most
polluting models to cover penalties.

207
00:10:31,990 --> 00:10:34,380
It might cut slow-selling models.

208
00:10:34,430 --> 00:10:38,040
Or it might slash prices to win
back buyers which helps new buyers,

209
00:10:38,090 --> 00:10:41,190
but can hurt the resale value
of the cars people already own.

210
00:10:41,957 --> 00:10:44,907
And then there's the group behind
three of the brands on this list:

211
00:10:44,957 --> 00:10:45,587
Stellantis.

212
00:10:45,637 --> 00:10:49,367
Stellantis owns Jeep,
Peugeot, Alfa Romeo and Fiat,

213
00:10:49,417 --> 00:10:53,607
along with Ram, and it imports Chinese
brand Leapmotor through a partnership.

214
00:10:53,657 --> 00:10:54,777
In June 2026,

215
00:10:54,827 --> 00:10:58,874
CarsGuide's Stephen Ottley wrote that
Leapmotor sales were up more than 112

216
00:10:58,924 --> 00:11:04,287
per cent, while Jeep, Peugeot, Alfa
Romeo and Fiat were all going backwards.

217
00:11:04,337 --> 00:11:05,967
He asked the question directly:

218
00:11:06,017 --> 00:11:09,407
"does Jeep have a long-term future in
Australia?" And he pointed out that

219
00:11:09,457 --> 00:11:12,987
Dodge and Chrysler had already left
and that General Motors said it was

220
00:11:13,037 --> 00:11:15,627
committed to Holden
right up until it wasn't.

221
00:11:15,677 --> 00:11:18,927
To be fair, Stellantis says
it's investing heavily globally,

222
00:11:18,977 --> 00:11:22,487
with plans for 60 new vehicles
and 50 updated models.

223
00:11:22,537 --> 00:11:25,867
And Jeep and Peugeot both say
they're staying in Australia.

224
00:11:25,917 --> 00:11:28,767
But the numbers make it easy
to see why owners are nervous.

225
00:11:28,817 --> 00:11:30,187
So who's filling the gap?

226
00:11:30,237 --> 00:11:31,597
Mostly the newer brands.

227
00:11:31,647 --> 00:11:36,287
CarExpert reported Leapmotor up more
than 150 per cent in the first half of

228
00:11:36,337 --> 00:11:39,597
2026, to 779 sales.

229
00:11:39,647 --> 00:11:45,227
CarsGuide reported that BYD's luxury
brand Denza had passed 1,600 sales since

230
00:11:45,277 --> 00:11:46,087
arriving.

231
00:11:46,137 --> 00:11:47,947
And more Chinese brands keep arriving,

232
00:11:47,997 --> 00:11:51,107
with new utes and SUVs planned for 2027.

233
00:11:51,157 --> 00:11:54,477
The market isn't shrinking the brands
at the top are simply changing.

234
00:11:55,300 --> 00:11:56,790
So here's the most important part.

235
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:00,730
What actually happens to you if
your car's brand leaves Australia?

236
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:01,940
First, your rights.

237
00:12:01,990 --> 00:12:04,490
Under the Australian Consumer Law,

238
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:07,910
manufacturers and importers must make
spare parts and repair facilities

239
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:11,750
reasonably available for a reasonable
time after you buy a product.

240
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:15,270
That doesn't disappear just because
a brand stops selling new cars.

241
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:18,050
Second, look at what
happened in real exits.

242
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:21,130
When General Motors announced
the end of Holden in 2020,

243
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:24,550
it said it would provide spare parts
and servicing for "at least the next 10

244
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:27,570
years," and that it would
cover all warranty commitments.

245
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:30,850
When Citroen stopped selling new cars
in Australia on the 1st of November

246
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:35,370
2024, it said servicing would continue
through its authorised service centres,

247
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:37,970
with genuine parts and
factory-trained staff,

248
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:40,610
and that the factory
warranty would continue.

249
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:42,650
What about finance and insurance?

250
00:12:42,700 --> 00:12:43,910
If your brand leaves,

251
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:47,730
your car loan doesn't change you still
owe the same amount, on the same terms.

252
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:49,970
Your insurance continues too.

253
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:54,250
But insurers price policies partly on
repair costs and parts availability,

254
00:12:54,300 --> 00:12:58,090
so if parts become harder to get, that
could show up in premiums over time.

255
00:12:58,140 --> 00:13:01,710
And if your car is on a novated
lease, the lease continues as normal.

256
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:05,910
The bigger question is the residual
value the lump sum you'll owe at the end.

257
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:08,910
If the car's market value falls
because the brand has left,

258
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:10,990
it could be worth less than the residual.

259
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:13,350
That's worth discussing with
your lease provider early.

260
00:13:13,400 --> 00:13:15,230
But here's the reality.

261
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:16,410
Even with promises,

262
00:13:16,460 --> 00:13:20,030
owners of departed brands often
report it gets harder over time.

263
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:22,570
Fewer dealers means longer
drives for servicing.

264
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:24,550
Some parts can take longer to arrive.

265
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:27,410
And independent mechanics may
be less familiar with the brand.

266
00:13:27,460 --> 00:13:30,090
And the biggest hit is
usually resale value.

267
00:13:30,140 --> 00:13:32,390
When a brand leaves,
or looks like it might,

268
00:13:32,440 --> 00:13:35,310
used buyers get nervous
and prices can fall.

269
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:38,420
That's often a bigger cost for
owners than any servicing problem.

270
00:13:39,192 --> 00:13:42,495
So if you own a car from one of
these brands, here's what to do now.

271
00:13:42,545 --> 00:13:44,522
Step one: don't panic-sell.

272
00:13:44,572 --> 00:13:46,802
None of these brands
has announced an exit.

273
00:13:46,852 --> 00:13:49,682
Selling in a hurry could
cost you more than waiting.

274
00:13:49,732 --> 00:13:52,382
Step two: check your
warranty and service plan.

275
00:13:52,432 --> 00:13:55,242
Write down exactly what's
covered and until when.

276
00:13:55,292 --> 00:13:56,742
Keep every service record,

277
00:13:56,792 --> 00:14:00,282
because a full service history matters
even more if the brand's future is

278
00:14:00,332 --> 00:14:01,402
uncertain.

279
00:14:01,452 --> 00:14:04,982
Step three: find your nearest
service centres not just one.

280
00:14:05,032 --> 00:14:06,362
If your dealer closes,

281
00:14:06,412 --> 00:14:09,042
you want to know where the
next authorised workshop is,

282
00:14:09,092 --> 00:14:12,862
and whether a good independent
mechanic in your area knows the brand.

283
00:14:12,912 --> 00:14:14,762
Step four: keep up with recalls.

284
00:14:14,812 --> 00:14:18,702
Check the Product Safety Australia
website for your car's make and model,

285
00:14:18,752 --> 00:14:22,302
and make sure any recall work is done
while the dealer network is still in

286
00:14:22,352 --> 00:14:23,322
place.

287
00:14:23,372 --> 00:14:25,722
Step five: think about common parts.

288
00:14:25,772 --> 00:14:27,622
If you're keeping the car for a long time,

289
00:14:27,672 --> 00:14:30,942
it's worth knowing how easy it
is to get things like brake pads,

290
00:14:30,992 --> 00:14:33,122
filters and windscreens for your model.

291
00:14:33,172 --> 00:14:37,022
Step six: if you're planning to
sell in the next year or two anyway,

292
00:14:37,072 --> 00:14:38,082
watch the market.

293
00:14:38,132 --> 00:14:40,662
If there's an official announcement
that a brand is leaving,

294
00:14:40,712 --> 00:14:42,442
used values can fall quickly.

295
00:14:42,492 --> 00:14:46,502
Being prepared with your service history
ready and a realistic valuation means

296
00:14:46,552 --> 00:14:48,422
you can act fast if you need to.

297
00:14:48,472 --> 00:14:50,802
And if you're buying a new
car from a struggling brand,

298
00:14:50,852 --> 00:14:52,502
use it to your advantage.

299
00:14:52,552 --> 00:14:53,522
Discounts can be big.

300
00:14:53,572 --> 00:14:56,422
But ask the questions we covered
in our Black Friday video:

301
00:14:56,472 --> 00:14:58,002
who stands behind the warranty,

302
00:14:58,052 --> 00:15:01,282
where is the nearest service centre,
and how fast can they get common parts?

303
00:15:01,332 --> 00:15:05,132
Then factor a lower resale value
into the price you're willing to pay.

304
00:15:05,897 --> 00:15:06,967
Before we wrap up,

305
00:15:07,017 --> 00:15:11,347
here are the five warning signs to
watch for in 2027 for any brand,

306
00:15:11,397 --> 00:15:12,767
not just the ones in this video.

307
00:15:12,817 --> 00:15:17,197
One: sales collapsing month after
month, not just one bad month.

308
00:15:17,247 --> 00:15:20,507
Two: dealers closing,
especially in regional areas.

309
00:15:20,557 --> 00:15:24,357
Three: a change of importer
or distributor like Peugeot.

310
00:15:24,407 --> 00:15:26,387
Four: the model range shrinking,

311
00:15:26,437 --> 00:15:30,287
with popular models paused or
axed and no clear replacements.

312
00:15:30,337 --> 00:15:33,987
And five: huge, ongoing discounts
on new cars just to move stock.

313
00:15:34,037 --> 00:15:37,257
One warning sign on its own
doesn't mean a brand is leaving.

314
00:15:37,307 --> 00:15:39,587
But when you see three or four together,

315
00:15:39,637 --> 00:15:42,747
it's time to pay attention
especially if you're about to buy.

316
00:15:42,797 --> 00:15:46,667
So, which car brands might
not survive 2027 in Australia?

317
00:15:46,717 --> 00:15:47,567
On the numbers,

318
00:15:47,617 --> 00:15:51,527
Jeep and Peugeot are under the most
pressure Jeep with sales down more than

319
00:15:51,577 --> 00:15:55,807
ninety per cent from its peak, and
Peugeot with its importer walking away.

320
00:15:55,857 --> 00:15:59,767
Jaguar has deliberately stopped selling
new cars while it reinvents itself.

321
00:15:59,817 --> 00:16:01,737
Alfa Romeo is going backwards.

322
00:16:01,787 --> 00:16:03,647
And while Mitsubishi, Subaru,

323
00:16:03,697 --> 00:16:05,887
Nissan and Mazda aren't going anywhere,

324
00:16:05,937 --> 00:16:08,767
they face falling sales and
big emissions penalties.

325
00:16:08,817 --> 00:16:11,667
But remember: none of these
brands has announced it's leaving,

326
00:16:11,717 --> 00:16:14,107
and several say they're
committed to Australia.

327
00:16:14,157 --> 00:16:15,747
In a market with around seventy brands,

328
00:16:15,797 --> 00:16:19,187
though, history tells us that not all
of them will still be here in a few

329
00:16:19,237 --> 00:16:19,927
years.

330
00:16:19,977 --> 00:16:21,547
If this helped, hit like,

331
00:16:21,597 --> 00:16:24,907
subscribe and share it with
someone who owns one of these cars.

332
00:16:24,957 --> 00:16:27,967
Tell me in the comments: which
brand do you think won't make it?

333
00:16:28,017 --> 00:16:29,737
And I'll see you in the next video.
